1
00:00:00,567 --> 00:00:01,400
<i> Please save my mommy.</i>

2
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:02,833
<i>- Yvette.
- Andrew, it's mom.</i>

3
00:00:02,967 --> 00:00:03,967
<i>They said mommy
is in the hospital.</i>

4
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,067
<i> Go see her.</i>

5
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:07,300
<i>Clarissa, why did you
leave her alone?</i>

6
00:00:07,433 --> 00:00:10,600
<i>I can't handle it if something
happens to Ma'am Rebecca.</i>

7
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:12,267
<i> Wait, why did mommy overdose?</i>

8
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,367
<i>- It looks intentional.
- She couldn't do that.</i>

9
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,733
<i>Do you believe what Tomas said?</i>

10
00:00:16,867 --> 00:00:18,467
<i>All of these are
just assumptions.</i>

11
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,967
<i>Your Korean friend,
do you still talk to him?</i>

12
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:22,567
<i> Who? Jin Ho?</i>

13
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:25,433
<i>That's not his real name
and he's not Korean police.</i>

14
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:28,000
<i>Does it bother you that you're
still not part of us?</i>

15
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:30,433
<i>I want to be in the room
where decisions are made.</i>

16
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:34,933
<i>Now, I'm burning bridges
for this organization!</i>

17
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,167
<i>Berdugo doesn't show himself
to ambition.</i>

18
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,400
<i>You want to meet him?
Prove you can wait.</i>

19
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:41,600
<i>You and Lisa will handle
the operations for now.</i>

20
00:00:41,733 --> 00:00:43,733
<i>So, you'll make it look like
suicide?</i>

21
00:00:43,867 --> 00:00:45,133
<i>Yes, that way there's
no complications.</i>

22
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:47,067
<i>Given her condition
and medications,</i>

23
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,733
<i>authorities will read it
as trauma or psychosis.</i>

24
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:50,767
<i> Tomas...</i>

25
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:14,633
I'll call you back.

26
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:16,300
Thank you.

27
00:01:16,433 --> 00:01:23,067
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

28
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,300
Andrew, what do you need?

29
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:27,767
Is that the hospital?

30
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:28,933
The what?

31
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,067
My sister told me

32
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,067
you were suggesting
a better hospital for mom.

33
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:39,233
Yes, a specialist.

34
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:42,400
Better equipped,
more experienced.

35
00:01:43,467 --> 00:01:46,833
I only want the best
for your mother's team.

36
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:48,333
Thank you, Tomas.

37
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:52,267
I'm really appreciative that...

38
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:53,867
you're doing this for mommy.

39
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,767
You don't have to thank me,
Andrew.

40
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,733
We're family, remember?

41
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:04,267
Tomas, you and Yvette
should go home.

42
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,400
I'll watch over mommy.

43
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:07,700
No, I'll stay.

44
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:09,200
The doctor might arrive.

45
00:02:09,333 --> 00:02:11,267
I need to speak
to her personally.

46
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:14,500
I'll just have the driver
take Yvette home.

47
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:24,433
[OMINOUS MUSIC CONTINUES]

48
00:02:35,567 --> 00:02:37,300
[PHONE CHIMES]

49
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:46,400
[PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

50
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:48,500
Mom, why? Who's that?

51
00:02:48,667 --> 00:02:51,767
One of the maids at the mansion.

52
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:56,300
She said Ma'am Rebecca
is comatose.

53
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:02,067
Mom, don't overthink too much.

54
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,000
No, dear.

55
00:03:04,133 --> 00:03:06,700
But you know,
what I'm thinking is,

56
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:10,200
Ma'am Rebecca isn't depressed.

57
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:15,767
Yes, she was often not
in the mood but not depressed.

58
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,967
Well, Mom, people can't really
assume that

59
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,200
because no one saw what really
happened.

60
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:26,733
<i> What are you doing?</i>

61
00:03:26,867 --> 00:03:28,300
<i> Can I help?</i>

62
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:29,700
<i> This...</i>

63
00:03:29,833 --> 00:03:34,400
<i>It's a spy camera attached to
the eye of the stuffed animal.</i>

64
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:36,200
The teddy bear...

65
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:37,400
Teddy bear?

66
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:39,533
Yes, child.

67
00:03:39,667 --> 00:03:42,000
In Ma'am Rebecca's room,
she had a teddy bear,

68
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:43,900
and it had a spy camera.

69
00:03:44,033 --> 00:03:47,033
I'm sure it recorded
what really happened.

70
00:03:47,167 --> 00:03:50,167
It would be good if we could get
that footage.

71
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:51,367
Yes.

72
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,200
Nanny, tie up the trash bags.

73
00:03:57,333 --> 00:03:58,767
Yes, Ma'am.

74
00:04:01,167 --> 00:04:03,867
[PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

75
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,533
<i>The brown stuffed bear I gave
her for her birthday...</i>

76
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:12,300
<i> can I see it?</i>

77
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:17,133
[PENSIVE MUSIC CONTINUES]

78
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:22,267
Mommy?

79
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:23,400
Sweetheart...

80
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:24,967
Don't come here.

81
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:26,633
It's dusty, child.

82
00:04:26,767 --> 00:04:29,167
Is grandmother coming home
already, Mommy?

83
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:35,900
Not yet, dear, but...

84
00:04:36,033 --> 00:04:39,233
if you pray harder, maybe.

85
00:04:39,367 --> 00:04:40,867
How about Aunt Clarissa?

86
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,833
Is she coming back when
grandmother comes back?

87
00:04:45,267 --> 00:04:48,400
Wait, child, okay?
I just have to do something.

88
00:04:52,567 --> 00:04:55,067
<i> Should I tell Ma'am Yvette?</i>

89
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:56,267
But, Mom,

90
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:58,533
we're not sure if the camera
in the teddy bear

91
00:04:58,667 --> 00:05:00,767
recorded anything.

92
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:02,433
You're right.

93
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:05,933
I don't want to add
to Ma'am Yvette's worries.

94
00:05:07,667 --> 00:05:11,333
But I'll just ask one of the
maids at the mansion for help.

95
00:05:15,567 --> 00:05:18,833
[PHONE RINGING]

96
00:05:18,967 --> 00:05:20,733
It's Ma'am Yvette.

97
00:05:20,867 --> 00:05:24,233
Answer it, but relax, Mom, okay?

98
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:28,567
Ma'am Yvette...

99
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,233
Hello, Clarissa?

100
00:05:30,367 --> 00:05:31,600
<i> How are you?</i>

101
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:33,033
I'm fine, Ma'am.

102
00:05:33,167 --> 00:05:35,467
<i> How are you?</i>

103
00:05:35,633 --> 00:05:40,133
We're still waiting
for mommy to wake up.

104
00:05:40,267 --> 00:05:42,133
<i> Clarissa, I've been thinking.</i>

105
00:05:42,267 --> 00:05:43,800
I'm sorry.

106
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:47,067
I was too emotional.

107
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:47,933
[PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

108
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:51,167
I'm sorry I shouted at you.

109
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:54,433
Ma'am, don't worry.

110
00:05:54,567 --> 00:05:59,433
I understand. It's normal...

111
00:05:59,567 --> 00:06:03,333
when something bad happens
to someone we love,

112
00:06:03,467 --> 00:06:06,633
we look for someone to blame.

113
00:06:08,267 --> 00:06:10,133
It happened to me too.

114
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:12,333
Thank you.

115
00:06:13,433 --> 00:06:15,767
Do you want to visit mommy?

116
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:18,400
Yes, of course.

117
00:06:18,533 --> 00:06:19,700
All right.

118
00:06:19,833 --> 00:06:21,333
We transferred her
to another hospital,

119
00:06:21,467 --> 00:06:23,600
<i>so I'll just text you
the address.</i>

120
00:06:23,733 --> 00:06:24,833
Okay.

121
00:06:26,267 --> 00:06:31,333
Ma'am, can I stop
by the mansion first?

122
00:06:31,467 --> 00:06:36,633
I left some things
in Ma'am Rebecca's room.

123
00:06:36,767 --> 00:06:37,733
Okay.

124
00:06:37,867 --> 00:06:40,733
I'll tell the maids
you're coming.

125
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:42,400
All right, thank you, Ma'am.

126
00:06:42,533 --> 00:06:46,000
I'll also get some clothes
for Ma'am Rebecca

127
00:06:46,133 --> 00:06:47,567
<i> to bring to the hospital.</i>

128
00:06:47,700 --> 00:06:51,100
Okay, thank you, Clarissa.

129
00:06:51,233 --> 00:06:52,933
Thank you, Ma'am.

130
00:06:54,433 --> 00:07:01,900
[PENSIVE MUSIC CONTINUES]

131
00:07:06,467 --> 00:07:08,167
The tip I got said

132
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,267
most of the people who have
their cars cleaned here

133
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,767
are Koreans,
because the owner is Korean.

134
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:13,967
I just thought...

135
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:16,367
Why would he go here?
It's all Koreans.

136
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:18,267
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

137
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:19,867
Because at the carwash,

138
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,967
they only notice the cars,
not the people.

139
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:26,367
What's your plan, Major?
Are you going inside?

140
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:32,000
[OMINOUS MUSIC CONTINUES]

141
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:33,967
Are you sure it was here

142
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:35,167
when something happened
to Ma'am Rebecca?

143
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:37,933
Yes, I last saw it here,

144
00:07:38,067 --> 00:07:41,267
but I don't know why it's gone
now.

145
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:44,967
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

146
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:47,167
So? Did you find
what you were looking for?

147
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:51,267
Ma'am, well...

148
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:58,000
Did you notice
a brown stuffed toy?

149
00:07:58,133 --> 00:07:59,167
A teddy bear.

150
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,633
The one for Kisses?

151
00:08:00,767 --> 00:08:02,733
Yes, yes.

152
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:05,367
I saw it earlier
when I was cleaning.

153
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:07,000
It was thrown in the trash.

154
00:08:07,133 --> 00:08:09,467
What?

155
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:16,733
This is where we threw
the trash.

156
00:08:17,667 --> 00:08:21,400
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

157
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:23,367
Where is it?

158
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:27,000
Are you sure you only throw
the trash here?

159
00:08:27,133 --> 00:08:28,467
Yes, just here.

160
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:31,100
Why is there no trash now?

161
00:08:31,233 --> 00:08:34,300
Maybe the garbage truck
passed by.

162
00:08:34,433 --> 00:08:36,600
Why would they throw you away?

163
00:08:36,733 --> 00:08:39,100
Good thing I found you
right away.

164
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:49,700
[INTRIGUING MUSIC PLAYING]

165
00:08:54,667 --> 00:09:02,100
[INTRIGUING MUSIC CONTINUES]

166
00:09:10,433 --> 00:09:13,367
Stay here first,
so no one can see you.

167
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,733
I won't let anyone
throw you away.

168
00:09:15,867 --> 00:09:17,333
Bye.

169
00:09:18,733 --> 00:09:20,533
It surely went straight
to the dump.

170
00:09:20,667 --> 00:09:21,867
If you go there,

171
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,667
you'll have a hard time
searching.

172
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,300
It's big, and it stinks.

173
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:29,300
- But if you want...
- No need.

174
00:09:29,467 --> 00:09:30,867
It's okay. Thank you.

175
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,267
All right. I'll leave you now.

176
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:33,633
I'll go back to what
I was doing.

177
00:09:33,767 --> 00:09:35,600
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

178
00:09:39,867 --> 00:09:41,167
So, what now, Mom?

179
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:45,433
Did Ma'am Rebecca tell you
why she put a spy camera?

180
00:09:45,567 --> 00:09:48,333
[STAMMERS]
Yes, but I couldn't...

181
00:09:48,467 --> 00:09:50,433
[STIRRING MUSIC PLAYING]

182
00:09:50,567 --> 00:09:53,600
Right, I remember now.

183
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:58,233
<i>It's a spy camera attached
to the eye of the stuffed toy.</i>

184
00:09:58,367 --> 00:10:00,033
<i>There's someone I need to catch.</i>

185
00:10:00,167 --> 00:10:04,700
<i>It needs to end
before my family gets hurt.</i>

186
00:10:04,867 --> 00:10:06,100
Is that true, Mom?

187
00:10:06,233 --> 00:10:10,067
Child, why would I lie?

188
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:13,933
I clearly remember because
when she told me that,

189
00:10:14,067 --> 00:10:17,533
I was so confused
about who she wanted to catch.

190
00:10:17,667 --> 00:10:20,267
The problem is,
I didn't get to ask her.

191
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:23,233
Why?

192
00:10:23,367 --> 00:10:26,767
Did you notice anything unusual
about her?

193
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:29,667
Maybe she has an enemy?
The maids?

194
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:33,333
No, the maids were kind.

195
00:10:33,467 --> 00:10:35,500
Then who could it be?

196
00:10:35,667 --> 00:10:37,133
Ma'am Yvette?

197
00:10:37,267 --> 00:10:38,800
Sir Tomas?

198
00:10:38,933 --> 00:10:40,833
[STIRRING MUSIC CONTINUES]

199
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:49,033
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

200
00:10:49,167 --> 00:10:50,267
Cruz,

201
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:51,667
is he there?

202
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:54,400
Negative, Major.

203
00:10:54,533 --> 00:10:57,300
Not here either. It's hard,
they all look alike.

204
00:10:57,433 --> 00:10:59,567
I told you, Major.

205
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:01,333
<i> Just wait there.</i>

206
00:11:01,467 --> 00:11:03,933
If he'll try to escape.
We can chase him.

207
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:07,433
Hello.

208
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:08,867
Indonesia?

209
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,167
[STAMMERS]

210
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:11,633
Vietnamese?

211
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:16,300
- Filipino.
- Yes.

212
00:11:16,433 --> 00:11:17,300
I knew it!

213
00:11:17,433 --> 00:11:18,733
Even if we're in the
Philippines,

214
00:11:18,867 --> 00:11:21,967
there are no Filipinos here.
Mostly foreigners.

215
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,433
You like the coffee, Sir?

216
00:11:23,567 --> 00:11:24,700
Yes.

217
00:11:24,833 --> 00:11:25,800
Nice! You know what?

218
00:11:25,933 --> 00:11:28,433
You're gonna love it
because we have a service.

219
00:11:28,567 --> 00:11:30,467
It's unlimited.

220
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:33,667
Anytime, you can come
to have a drink, Sir.

221
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:35,633
Don't Filipinos love coffee?

222
00:11:35,767 --> 00:11:36,767
Yes.

223
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:38,500
I'm going to eat.

224
00:11:38,633 --> 00:11:39,667
I see.

225
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,167
I'm sorry.

226
00:11:42,333 --> 00:11:43,067
Hey!

227
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:45,067
[CLAPS]
A Filipino client here!

228
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:46,433
It's been so long!

229
00:11:46,567 --> 00:11:47,433
Yes, Sir!

230
00:11:47,567 --> 00:11:49,533
- Okay, very good. Here, Sir.
- Thank you!

231
00:11:50,567 --> 00:11:51,933
What was that, Major?

232
00:11:52,067 --> 00:11:53,333
<i> Be careful, Major.</i>

233
00:11:53,467 --> 00:11:55,267
<i>You might draw
too much attention.</i>

234
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:56,600
Remember...

235
00:11:56,733 --> 00:11:59,833
The syndicate thinks
you're dead.

236
00:11:59,967 --> 00:12:01,533
<i> Focus on the shop.</i>

237
00:12:01,667 --> 00:12:03,833
Standby there, okay? Stay alert.

238
00:12:03,967 --> 00:12:08,700
Did Ma'am Rebecca tell you
not to trust Sir Tomas?

239
00:12:08,833 --> 00:12:10,133
Why?

240
00:12:11,367 --> 00:12:12,533
I don't know...

241
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:15,267
She kept warning me.

242
00:12:15,433 --> 00:12:17,467
<i> A person too private...</i>

243
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:21,400
<i> usually has many secrets.</i>

244
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:22,900
<i> Just remember...</i>

245
00:12:23,033 --> 00:12:26,267
<i> Do not trust Tomas.</i>

246
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:27,733
What do you think, dear?

247
00:12:27,867 --> 00:12:30,667
Should I tell Ma'am Yvette?

248
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:33,300
Anyway, we're okay now.

249
00:12:33,433 --> 00:12:36,400
But, Mom, it's risky.

250
00:12:36,533 --> 00:12:39,333
He's still Ma'am Yvette's
husband.

251
00:12:39,467 --> 00:12:42,167
And we don't have
a solid evidence.

252
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:44,167
True.

253
00:12:44,300 --> 00:12:46,967
Ma'am Rebecca
is in comatose too.

254
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:50,333
She can't confirm anything
I say.

255
00:12:52,967 --> 00:12:55,200
What if...

256
00:12:55,333 --> 00:12:57,633
You tell Sir Andrew instead?

257
00:12:58,667 --> 00:13:01,900
I'm sure he'll understand.

258
00:13:02,033 --> 00:13:04,500
It's for his mother.

259
00:13:04,633 --> 00:13:07,667
[STIRRING MUSIC PLAYING]

260
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:15,167
[LIFE MONITOR BEEPING]

261
00:13:18,067 --> 00:13:19,867
<i>She's been a doctor
for 25 years.</i>

262
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,733
She specializes
in neurocritical care,

263
00:13:22,900 --> 00:13:25,667
so she can give us
the right explanation

264
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:27,800
about your mother's case.

265
00:13:27,933 --> 00:13:30,533
I reviewed Rebecca's case
thoroughly...

266
00:13:30,667 --> 00:13:33,600
And she suffered a prolonged
hypoxic episode

267
00:13:33,733 --> 00:13:35,533
due to drug toxicity.

268
00:13:35,667 --> 00:13:37,933
The damage is significant.

269
00:13:39,033 --> 00:13:40,933
But she's stable, right?

270
00:13:41,067 --> 00:13:42,800
Medically, yes.

271
00:13:42,933 --> 00:13:47,067
But recovery isn't just about
stability.

272
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:51,133
She has reflexes, yes,
but no meaningful response.

273
00:13:51,267 --> 00:13:54,267
You mean she has no chance
of waking up?

274
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:56,033
Of course, there is.

275
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:58,767
But the longer she remains
unresponsive,

276
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:01,233
the narrower the chance becomes.

277
00:14:01,367 --> 00:14:04,167
Thank you, Doc.
We appreciate your honesty.

278
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:06,300
I'll leave you for now.

279
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:15,767
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

280
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:17,700
Get some rest.

281
00:14:17,833 --> 00:14:20,733
The nurses are watching over
your mother.

282
00:14:22,867 --> 00:14:24,333
Let's go?

283
00:14:25,833 --> 00:14:31,367
[OMINOUS MUSIC CONTINUES]

284
00:14:32,700 --> 00:14:33,867
Andrew.

285
00:14:38,967 --> 00:14:41,833
Leave him be. Let's go.

286
00:14:50,167 --> 00:14:56,633
[LIFE MONITOR BEEPING]

287
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:02,233
[DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING]

288
00:15:04,333 --> 00:15:05,500
Mom,

289
00:15:05,633 --> 00:15:07,433
wake up please.

290
00:15:08,633 --> 00:15:10,767
I know you can hear me.

291
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:16,300
I know that you always could
even when you didn't want to.

292
00:15:18,367 --> 00:15:20,933
That's what you always
told me before, right?

293
00:15:22,267 --> 00:15:24,667
<i> You always sense things</i>

294
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:29,400
<i>and you always know
what's happening around you</i>

295
00:15:29,533 --> 00:15:31,800
<i>even when you pretended not to.</i>

296
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:34,333
<i> That's why I know...</i>

297
00:15:35,467 --> 00:15:38,067
you couldn't do this
to yourself, Mom.

298
00:15:38,200 --> 00:15:39,600
You're brave, aren't you?

299
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,633
You don't give up.

300
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:46,433
This isn't you, Mom.

301
00:15:48,033 --> 00:15:51,533
This doesn't make sense.

302
00:15:52,900 --> 00:16:02,867
[DRAMATIC MUSIC CONTINUES]

303
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:15,967
Cruz,

304
00:16:16,100 --> 00:16:18,467
they're almost done cleaning
my car.

305
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:20,267
<i> Still no Jin Ho.</i>

306
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:22,667
Are you sure your informant
said he hangs out here?

307
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:25,733
He wouldn't give weak intel,
Major.

308
00:16:25,867 --> 00:16:28,600
Maybe the target just isn't
coming here today.

309
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:37,133
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

310
00:16:37,267 --> 00:16:39,000
Jin Ho?

311
00:16:39,133 --> 00:16:45,267
[OMINOUS MUSIC CONTINUES]

312
00:16:47,600 --> 00:16:52,100
[BRAZEN MUSIC PLAYING]

313
00:16:52,233 --> 00:16:53,733
[CAR CHIMES]

314
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:09,967
Why are you following me?

315
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:11,800
Are you a pervert?

316
00:17:11,933 --> 00:17:13,400
Are you gay?

317
00:17:13,533 --> 00:17:15,033
No, no.

318
00:17:16,100 --> 00:17:24,933
[OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

319
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:31,767
[BRAZEN MUSIC PLAYING]

320
00:17:33,233 --> 00:17:34,533
Jin Ho...

321
00:17:35,567 --> 00:17:37,067
Where did he go?

322
00:17:40,033 --> 00:17:41,633
I don't know if...

323
00:17:42,767 --> 00:17:45,167
I should start working.

324
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:46,267
Because...

325
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:48,433
I just keep overthinking.

326
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:52,067
But I can't focus.

327
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:55,967
Just call me when you need
someone to talk to, okay?

328
00:17:56,100 --> 00:17:58,267
And don't worry about
the "Tirada," Boss.

329
00:17:58,400 --> 00:17:59,733
I can do what you do too.

330
00:17:59,867 --> 00:18:01,167
Look.
[CLEARS THROAT]

331
00:18:02,267 --> 00:18:06,433
I'm Kyle Cañete,
striking for the truth.

332
00:18:06,567 --> 00:18:10,067
[CHEEKY MUSIC PLAYING]
Anytime, anywhere.

333
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:12,267
That was good, right?
See? Right?

334
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:13,767
How do you do it it again? How?
Again, again, again.

335
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:14,800
[ELAI LAUGHS]

336
00:18:14,933 --> 00:18:18,667
I'm cooler
by about 5 points, Boss.

337
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:20,100
All right.

338
00:18:20,233 --> 00:18:21,467
You only dream once
in a while anyway.

339
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:23,100
[KYLE CHUCKLES]
So, you agree?

340
00:18:23,267 --> 00:18:25,133
We'll lose our subscribers.

341
00:18:25,267 --> 00:18:26,333
Hey, wow, harsh.

342
00:18:26,467 --> 00:18:27,533
They'll increase.

343
00:18:27,667 --> 00:18:30,200
They'll grow to 1 million.

344
00:18:30,333 --> 00:18:33,033
[INDISTINCT CHATTER]

345
00:18:36,433 --> 00:18:37,567
Hi, guys.

346
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:39,400
- Hello.
- Hi, Zoey.

347
00:18:39,533 --> 00:18:41,567
All right, Andrew,
we'll go ahead.

348
00:18:41,700 --> 00:18:43,267
- Text us.
- Boss...

349
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:44,433
Me, okay?

350
00:18:44,567 --> 00:18:46,867
- Take care.
- Boss, what I said, okay?

351
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:48,867
Start moving.

352
00:18:50,933 --> 00:18:57,533
[MELLOW MUSIC PLAYING]

353
00:18:59,267 --> 00:19:00,500
How are you?

354
00:19:02,100 --> 00:19:03,600
How's Auntie?

355
00:19:05,267 --> 00:19:06,533
How did you know?

356
00:19:06,667 --> 00:19:08,200
From Yvette.

357
00:19:08,333 --> 00:19:10,933
You know, Andrew,
just because we broke up

358
00:19:11,067 --> 00:19:15,967
doesn't mean I cut off all your
connections in my life.

359
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:17,500
And...

360
00:19:17,633 --> 00:19:20,700
When I heard what happened
to Auntie Rebecca,

361
00:19:20,833 --> 00:19:22,900
I knew you needed someone.

362
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,300
That's why I'm here.
I'm here.

363
00:19:26,433 --> 00:19:29,000
Just like before when something
happened to her too.

364
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:33,267
No, you weren't there.

365
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:34,733
You left.

366
00:19:35,567 --> 00:19:37,800
[MELLOW MUSIC CONTINUES]

367
00:19:40,367 --> 00:19:44,067
<i>Honestly, it's so hard
to see our mother like that.</i>

368
00:19:46,100 --> 00:19:48,400
Good thing I'm not alone.

369
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:52,100
My children are here...

370
00:19:53,733 --> 00:19:57,867
Tomas, and of course, Andrew.

371
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:02,333
When I started thinking that...

372
00:20:02,500 --> 00:20:08,700
maybe mommy did think of doing
this to herself...

373
00:20:08,833 --> 00:20:10,967
but thank God,

374
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:14,700
Andrew was able to knock
some sense into me.

375
00:20:14,833 --> 00:20:18,100
Because he's the one who knows
mommy better.

376
00:20:18,233 --> 00:20:20,767
[PENSIVE MUSIC PLAYING]

377
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:23,700
Ma'am, is Andrew here now?

378
00:20:23,833 --> 00:20:26,200
Yeah, he's in the garden.

379
00:20:26,333 --> 00:20:28,100
I'll just go see him. Excuse me.

380
00:20:28,233 --> 00:20:29,633
All right.

381
00:20:32,533 --> 00:20:34,267
You, Clarissa,

382
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:37,200
do you think mommy
could commit suicide?

383
00:20:37,367 --> 00:20:39,033
Ma'am...

384
00:20:39,167 --> 00:20:40,800
There's...

385
00:20:40,933 --> 00:20:42,800
There's something I should
tell you.

386
00:20:42,933 --> 00:20:44,833
Yvette.
[SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC PLAYING]

387
00:20:46,933 --> 00:20:48,233
Clarissa.

388
00:20:50,367 --> 00:20:53,033
What were you going to say,
Clarissa?

389
00:20:55,900 --> 00:20:58,067
Is everything okay?

390
00:20:58,200 --> 00:20:59,433
Yes, Sir.

391
00:21:00,467 --> 00:21:01,300
Nothing, Ma'am.

392
00:21:01,433 --> 00:21:04,600
What I was going to say
isn't important.

393
00:21:04,733 --> 00:21:06,467
I'll just say it another time.

394
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:08,967
All right.

395
00:21:09,133 --> 00:21:16,667
[SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC CONTINUES]

396
00:21:18,067 --> 00:21:22,833
[LIFE MONITOR BEEPING]

397
00:21:24,567 --> 00:21:31,767
[UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING]

398
00:21:31,900 --> 00:21:33,933
Don't forget my advice, okay?

399
00:21:34,067 --> 00:21:36,333
I won't be like you.

400
00:21:36,467 --> 00:21:37,400
What's this?

401
00:21:37,533 --> 00:21:39,533
I got it from inside there.

402
00:21:39,667 --> 00:21:41,267
Can you help us,
Uncle Andrew?

403
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,100
Okay, what is this?

404
00:21:43,267 --> 00:21:44,433
Let's see.

405
00:21:44,567 --> 00:21:46,367
Yvette, I still can't imagine
how this happened to mommy.

406
00:21:46,500 --> 00:21:49,033
I know for myself she couldn't
do this to herself.

407
00:21:49,167 --> 00:21:50,800
So, who? Who would do this?

408
00:21:50,933 --> 00:21:52,567
The only reason I agreed
to come with you

409
00:21:52,700 --> 00:21:54,933
is to see if Jin Ho
is still here.

410
00:21:55,067 --> 00:21:57,000
Emman!

411
00:21:57,133 --> 00:21:59,033
This is about your friend,
Jin Ho.

412
00:21:59,167 --> 00:22:00,167
He's a criminal.

413
00:22:00,300 --> 00:22:02,867
If he's been in the syndicate
for a long time,

414
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,567
why doesn't his name appear
in our files?

415
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:06,733
Come.

416
00:22:07,767 --> 00:22:09,633
It's not impossible
for the syndicate to doubt

417
00:22:09,767 --> 00:22:11,500
as long as they don't see
your body.

418
00:22:11,633 --> 00:22:13,600
We'll stand by the claim
that I'm dead.
